  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1253 PM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nA 500mb trough extends from the Eastern Great Lakes southwestward \nacross KY and into AR currently. Forcing associated with the trough \nhas produced areas of drizzle and a few light showers that have \nprimarily been impacting areas east of the MS River. The trough will \nshift southeastward tonight into Monday, with the southern extent \npositioned more into east TN and GA. Patchy to areas of fog may \ndevelop tonight as skies clear out. We should stay dry on Monday \nalong with a return to some sunshine helping to boost temps back \ninto the low to mid 80s. The upper energy to our southeast may \nattempt to lift back northwest some Tuesday and Wednesday, which \ncould allow a few showers or isolated storms to sneak back into the \nKY Pennyrile. Seems most likely to remain to our south and east, but \ncan't completely rule them out (10-20% chance). Overall looks like a \ndry next 3 days for the majority of our region.\n\nAs northern stream troughiness develops across the Upper Midwest, it \nwill allow ripples of energy to dive southeastward into the Midwest \nand Great Lakes from Wednesday night through Saturday. This may \nresult in a somewhat unsettled period for portions of our region, \nalthough current PoPs are only in the 20-30% range at best. The main \nfrontal boundary may remain north of our region most of this time, \nbut it may come close enough to produce some higher chances for \nconvection. Models suggest the highest wind shear (25 kts or \ngreater) largely stays north of our area, but with instability \nlikely to exceed 2000 j/kg a few stronger storms can't be ruled out \nduring this time period. ML severe probs jump back to 5% for \nThursday through Saturday, although highest probs stay north of us. \nSomething to monitor at least, and given how active this summer has \nbeen, wouldn't be surprising at all to see some stronger convection \nreturn. \n\nWhile temperatures moderate back to near seasonal values mid to late \nweek, it looks like we may hold off until the Aug 9-11 period for \nour next potential heatwave. LREF probabilities of hitting 90 are in \nthe 50-60% range by Thurs-Sat across southern portions of our region \n(even as early as Wed in our far west), but they jump up to 60-80% \nby next Sun-Tues. Ensembles are suggesting upper heights rise during \nthis time as ridging tries to build back into the mid Mississippi \nValley. NBM has toned down heat index readings for late week, \nprimarily mid to upper 90s, but has them jumping back to 100 in \nparts of the area by Saturday and most of our area by next Sunday. \nSome guidance indicates this heat could continue through much of \nnext week, with the ECMWF being the most bullish with it.",
